In article <1992Feb26.122209.15941@yang.earlham.edu>, marcs@yang.earlham.edu (Thunderbolt (aka. Marc Seebass)) writes:
>The other day, I had used a scroll of genocied to kill off the soldiers on 
>the level, and I found a barraks with loads of weapons, and I thought 
>wouldn't it be neat if I could set up a shop if I ever decided to quit 
>that game. How about allowing adventurers to set up shops, and thus become 
>computer controlled characters.- 

Only a strong character should be allowed to set up a shop, or else the game
could be filled up with shops too easy to rob.  Most rooms have more than
one door, so the characters should also have enough pets to guard the extra
doors.  

An interesting question is how the score should be computed for a player that
decides to set up a shop.
Should he get extra points if he managed to set up a large shop with lots
of fine things?  Should his score increase later if he runs the shop well,
(i.e. earns a lot, kills thiefs and take their possessions), and if so, should 
it decrease when he get robbed?

What if the player already has the amulet?  A serious player would of course
try to finish the game, but surely someone will try to set up a shop on
level 2...
